Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 08:52:44 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 11240/11362] A checkpoint no longer publishes a file under an
 open mutation proof (REQ-INFRA-6977)

A mutation proof breaks a tracked file on purpose, confirms the suite goes RED,
then restores it byte-identically. Between those steps the file on disk is wrong.
The conductor's checkpoint commits whatever is dirty every few minutes and runs no
hooks, so on 2026-09-05 it published a deliberately-broken module to main, where it
stood for 3 minutes 47 seconds.

Operator approved the middle form: let the checkpoint proceed, but exclude paths
named by an open proof. Two staging paths need it, because they do not share code.
git_commit_and_push goes through _stage_all_except_claimed and is covered by
unstaging from the index; the interrupted-run checkpoint in research_step stages
file-by-file and never reaches that function, so it filters its own list.

The lock path is imported from the module that owns it, never copied. It lives
under --git-common-dir so every worktree shares one, and the ops/ copy written here
first would have missed it in the normal case while still looking like a guard.

The two fail directions differ and both are deliberate. Reading the lock fails
OPEN and logs: the checkpoint exists to preserve in-flight work, and losing that is
unrecoverable while a published broken file is one revert away. The empty case
fails CLOSED: if the broken file is the only dirty path there is nothing to
preserve, and the only alternative is publishing it.

Mutation proofs, under an exclusive session, each restored byte-identically by cmp:
rule deleted RED (2 tests), exclusion removed from _stage_all_except_claimed RED,
git restore never issued RED, helper rule deleted RED (2 tests). One survived and
is stated rather than hidden: research_step ignoring the helper's return value
leaves the suite green, because reaching that line needs a full conductor fixture.
The decision was extracted out of research_step so the logic is provable and the
unproven surface is one assignment. Named in the spec's honest-limit paragraph.

Two deliberate overrides, both explained rather than worked around:

--mutation-force-unlock closed the session. The gate compares the target to its
bytes at --mutation-begin, and this file legitimately changed during the session
when the helper was extracted. No mutation was left in the tree; restores were
verified by cmp at each step and the 13 tests are green.

A pending marker was deleted deliberately. It was armed by this file's own pytest
run and named openspec/capabilities/pipeline/spec.md and scripts/research_conductor.py,
both tagged [NOT this run] -- that is, authored edits present before the run, not
test damage. Reverting them is the destruction this guard has caused three times.
The rewrite is intended and is what this commit contains.

